    <h1>About this letter</h1>

    <p>
      This is an open letter asking that the U.S. Web Design System (USWDS) be
      kept funded and maintained. It was written by former federal employees who
      helped build and scale it, and it is open to anyone else who wants to sign.
    </p>

    <h2>Why USWDS matters</h2>

    <p>
      USWDS is a shared component library. When agencies build on it, a fix to a
      button or a color contrast problem happens once and every dependent site
      gets it. Without it, each agency reinvents the same components, and the
      accessibility work is repeated, or skipped.
    </p>

    <h2>How signing works</h2>

    <p>
      Signing means adding your name to a list. It does not mean your employer
      endorses the letter. If that distinction matters for your situation, say
      so in your role line, which appears under your name.
    </p>

    <p>
      Every signature goes through a pull request, so what is claimed is what
      someone wrote. The letter text itself is protected: a pull request that
      changes the letter fails CI unless a maintainer reviews it and applies a
      label.
    </p>

    <h2>How this site works</h2>

    <p>
      The site is a static build. There is no database, no account system, and no
      JavaScript. Signatures live in a plain YAML file in a public repository, and
      the site rebuilds from it. That means the full list of signatories is
      readable without this site, and stays readable if the site goes away.
    </p>

    <p>
      The letter is also published as
      <a class="usa-link" href="/letter.txt" download>a plain-text file</a> and
      as <a class="usa-link" href="/archive.json">JSON</a>, so it can be copied,
      archived, and verified independently of this site's styling.
    </p>

    <h2>The design system</h2>

    <p>
      This site is styled with USWDS, the same system the letter is about. The
      version is pinned exactly in the repository, and the stylesheet is compiled
      from USWDS source rather than copied from a published build, so what ships is
      traceable to a specific release.
    </p>

    <p>
      USWDS is a public-domain work of the United States government. Using it here
      is not an endorsement by any federal agency, and this site is not a
      government website.
    </p>
